  "spec": "RFC 6901 3 and Format 7.3: a JSON Pointer starts with / after the #, and in it ~ is written ~0 and / is written ~1. A pointer that breaks either is not a pointer, so it is reported as malformed rather than as reaching nothing (Format 7: tools MUST report invalid JSON Pointer syntax). The fix writes the pointer correctly, and is offered only when that reaches something: in a value, as a token, and for a group",
